[j], a[k]) from array a where i < j < k,\n    and a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is a multiple of 3.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _nC3(num):\n        \"\"\"Helper to compute binomial coefficient C(num, 3).\"\"\"\n        if num < 3:\n            return 0\n        return (num * (num - 1) * (num - 2)) // 6\n\n    # Analyze a[i] = i*i - i + 1 modulo 3:\n    # If i % 3 == 0: a[i] % 3 = (0*0 - 0 + 1) % 3 = 1\n    # If i % 3 == 1: a[i] % 3 = (1*1 - 1 + 1) % 3 = 1\n    # If i % 3 == 2: a[i] % 3 = (2*2 - 2 + 1) % 3 = (4 - 2 + 1) % 3 = 0\n    # So, a[i] % 3 is 0 if i % 3 == 2, and 1 otherwise.\n\n    # Count how many i (from 1 to n) have i % 3 == 2.\n    # These are indices 2, 5, 8, ...\n    base = n // 3\n    remainder = n % 3\n    count_i_mod_2 = base + (1 if remainder >= 2 else 0)\n\n    # count_a0 is the number of elements a[x] such that a[x] % 3 == 0.\n    # This corresponds to the count of indices where i % 3 == 2.\n    count_a0 = count_i_mod_2\n\n    # count_a1 is the number of elements a[x] such that a[x] % 3 == 1.\n    # This is the total elements n minus those that are 0 mod 3.\n    count_a1 = n - count_a0\n\n    # The sum a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is a multiple of 3 if and only if\n    # (a[i]%3 + a[j]%3 + a[k]%3) % 3 == 0.\n    # Since a[x]%3 can only be 0 or 1, the only valid combinations of residues are:\n    # 1. (0, 0, 0): sum = 0, which is 0 mod 3.\n    # 2. (1, 1, 1): sum = 3, which is 0 mod 3.\n    # Thus, we need to choose three elements all from the '0 mod 3' group,\n    # or three elements all from the '1 mod 3' group.\n\n    triples_from_a0 = _nC3(count_a0)\n    triples_from_a1 = _nC3(count_a1)\n\n    return triples_from_a0 + triples_from_a1", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}, {"task_id": "HumanEval/147", "solution": "def get_max_triples(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    You are given a positive integer n. You have to create an integer array a of length n.\n        For each i (1 \u2264 i \u2264 n), the value of a[i] = i * i - i + 1.\n        Return the number of triples (a[i], a[j], a[k]) of a where i < j < k, \n    and a[i] + a[j] + a[k] is a multiple of 3.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Helper function to compute \"n choose k\" for k=3.\n    # Returns 0 if count is less than k.\n    def _comb(count, k):\n        if count < k:\n            return 0\n        if k == 3:\n            return count * (count - 1) * (count - 2) // 6\n        return 0\n\n    if n < 3:\n        return 0\n\n    # We need to count the number of elements a[i] whose residue modulo 3 is 0, 1, or 2.\n    # Let's analyze a[i] = i*i - i + 1 modulo 3:\n    # - If i % 3 == 0: a[i] % 3 = (0*0 - 0 + 1) % 3 = 1\n    # - If i % 3 == 1: a[i] % 3 = (1*1 - 1 + 1) % 3 = 1\n    # - If i % 3 == 2: a[i] % 3 = (2*2 - 2 + 1) % 3 = (4 - 2 + 1) % 3 = 3 % 3 = 0\n    #\n    # So, a[i] % 3 will always be either 0 or 1. Residue 2 never appears.\n\n    # c0: count of a[i] such that a[i] % 3 == 0. This happens when i % 3 == 2.\n    # The values of i from 1 to n for which i % 3 == 2 are 2, 5, 8, ...\n    # The count of such numbers can be calculated as (n + 1) // 3.\n    c0 = (n + 1) // 3\n\n    # c1: count of a[i] such that a[i] % 3 == 1. This happens when i % 3 == 0 or i % 3 == 1.\n    # Since all a[i] % 3 are either 0 or 1, c1 is simply n - c0.\n    c1 = n - c0\n\n    # c2: count of a[i] such that a[i] % 3 == 2. This is always 0.\n    c2 = 0\n\n    # A triple (a[i], a[j], a[k]) has a sum divisible by 3 if the sum of their residues modulo 3 is 0.\n    # Possible combinations of residues (r_i, r_j, r_k) whose sum is 0 (mod 3):\n    # 1. (0, 0, 0): Number of ways = C(c0, 3)\n    # 2. (1, 1, 1): Number of ways = C(c1, 3)\n    # 3. (2, 2, 2): Number of ways = C(c2, 3)  (which is 0 because c2 is 0)\n    # 4. (0, 1, 2): Number of ways = c0 * c1 * c2 (which is 0 because c2 is 0)\n\n    total_triples = _comb(c0, 3) + _comb(c1, 3) + _comb(c2, 3) + (c0 * c1 * c2)\n\n    return total_triples", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus